

1./*
1. SECTION, Concept in my own words

I see a Struct as a "custom container" that allows me to group different types of information that belong together. Before this, if I wanted to store information
about a student, I had to create separate variables for their ID, their Name, and their GPA. It was a mess! Now, with a Struct, I can create a single type called 
Student that holds all those details inside. It’s like creating a digital ID card. I’ve learned that Structs are "Value Types," which means they behave similarly
to integers or floats—they store the actual data directly, and you have to make sure they have values assigned before you try to use them.

2. SECTION, Key C# Syntax

The way we define and use Structs is very organized. We usually define the structure outside of our main logic so it can be used anywhere in the program.

We use the struct keyword and follow PascalCase for the name and its members. We also use the public keyword so the information is accessible.

EXAMPLE:
*/
struct Student
{
    public long Id;  
    public string Name;
    public float GPA;
}

// 1. 
Student s1;
s1.Id = 202600001;
s1.Name = "Jane Smith";
s1.GPA = 4.0f;

Student[] ap1Students = new Student[10]; 
ap1Students[0] = s1; // Storing the first student in the first slot

void DisplayStudentInfo(Student aStudent) 
{
    Console.WriteLine($"ID: {aStudent.Id}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Name: {aStudent.Name}");
    Console.WriteLine($"GPA: {aStudent.GPA}");
}
/*

PascalCase: As the professor taught us, both the Struct name and its internal fields (Id, Name) should start with a capital letter.

Value Types: Unlike objects that might point to a location in memory, structs contain the values themselves.

Encapsulation: It allows us to pass a whole "package" of data to functions like DisplayStudentInfo or return a package from functions like FillStudentInfo().


3. SECTION, Eureka Exercise/Moment

a) What was confusing at first?

At first, I didn't get why I couldn't just print the whole struct by saying Console.WriteLine(s1). I expected it to show me all the data automatically, but instead,
it just showed the name of the Struct (System.Student). It was also confusing to realize that even though it feels like an object, I have to manually initialize every
single field inside it before I can use it, or the compiler will complain.

b) What mistake were you making?

A big mistake I made was trying to access a field that I hadn't assigned a value to yet. For example, if I only set the Name but forgot the Id, the program wouldn't 
let me pass that student to a function. I also struggled with the syntax of arrays of structs; I kept thinking I had to do something special, but it's actually the same
new Student[10] syntax we used for basic arrays.

c) What finally made sense?

Everything clicked for me when we did the Linear Search to find students with a GPA higher than 4.0. Before using structs, I would have had to manage three different
arrays and try to keep them all synced up, which was a nightmare. But seeing how I could just go through the ap1Students array and access the specific data I needed 
with a simple dot, like ap1Students[i].GPA, made the whole logic feel so much more professional and organized.

I also had a "lightbulb moment" when I realized that a Struct is a Value Type. At first, I was confused because I thought they were like arrays, but then I saw that
if I didn't manually assign a value to each field (like the Id or the Name), the program wouldn't even let me print them. Using functions like FillStudentInfo() and 
DisplayStudentInfo() really showed me the power of "packaging" data; instead of passing three separate variables to a function, I was just passing one single Student
object that had everything inside. It made me realize that programming is much easier when you group things that belong together instead of keeping them scattered 
everywhere.

4. SECTION, Describe one common mistake you made with this concept and why it happened. 

The most common error is forgetting that Structs are Value Types. If you create an array of Structs but don't fill the slots, they contain default values
(like 0 for numbers), but if you try to use an uninitialized single struct variable, your code won't even run. Also, forgetting the public keyword inside the
struct definition is a classic mistake—without it, you won't be able to access the Name or Id from your main program.

